id=EX;done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''In rank one with $d_i=1$, $\Lambda=\lambda\Lambda_1$ and the STANDARD first relation $x_1^\lambda=0$:
(a) $R^\Lambda(0)=k$. At root $\alpha_i$, $R^\Lambda(\alpha_i)=k[x]/x^\lambda$, of graded dimension $1+q^2+\cdots+q^{2(\lambda-1)}$ if $\lambda\ge1$. For $\lambda=0$ this block is zero and has NO simple. For $\lambda\ge1$ its unique normalized simple is $k$; its normalized projective HOM-self-dual cover is the regular module. The coefficient-of-$x^{\lambda-1}$ Frobenius FUNCTIONAL has degree $-2(\lambda-1)$.
(b) At root $n\alpha_i$ the cyclotomic nil-Hecke quotient is a graded matrix algebra over the Grassmannian coinvariant ring and is zero for $n>\lambda$. For $\lambda=2,n=2$, it is $\operatorname{End}_k(C\{-1\})$, where $C=k[x_1,x_2]/(x_1+x_2,x_1x_2)$ has basis $1,x_1$ in degrees $0,2$. Thus its unique self-dual simple $S_2=C\{-1\}$ has graded dimension $q^{-1}+q$; it is also the normalized indecomposable projective $P_2$. In particular it is NOT a one-dimensional module with both dots zero. The regular module satisfies $[R^\Lambda(2\alpha_i)]=(q+q^{-1})[P_2]$ in $K_0$, and the same formula with $S_2$ in $G_0$.
(c) Arbitrary monic $a_i$ gives the ungraded deformation: at $\lambda=2$, $a_i=u(u-1)$ gives $k\oplus k$ in the first block. This preserves finite dimension and ordinary symmetry but is not the standard graded $V(2)$ categorification.''',r'''1. The one-strand presentation has only the polynomial generator, so quotienting gives the displayed truncated ring. Its homogeneous ideals are powers of $x$; the sole simple for positive $\lambda$ is the degree-zero quotient and its regular cover has projective Hom dual equal to itself. At $\lambda=0$ the relation is $1=0$ and no simple exists. The functional is nonzero precisely in input degree $2(\lambda-1)$ and takes values in degree zero, hence has the NEGATIVE displayed degree; the anti-diagonal monomial pairing proves perfectness in every characteristic.
2. Use the explicit nil-Hecke endomorphism and divided-power supplier. The pure-first-power ideal becomes the Grassmannian complete-symmetric ideal in the symmetric corner; its determinant/monic reduction is the rank-one finite-string computation, with no factorial division. At $n=\lambda=2$ this corner is $k$, and the polynomial column specializes to the two-dimensional coinvariant $C$. In $C$, $x_2=-x_1$ and $x_1^2=0$; the divided difference and multiplication act on its two basis vectors as the matrix units, also in characteristic two. Centering this column by $-1$ gives its self-dual simple and cover.
3. The two polynomial-column shifts of the regular matrix algebra are $+1$ and $-1$. Therefore its normalized projective and simple classes both have coefficient $q+q^{-1}$. This preserves the correct regular multiplicity. The one-dimensional zero-dot proposal contradicts the equal-color mixed relation $\tau x_2-x_1\tau=-1$ in the native convention, so cannot be a module over a nonzero block.
4. For $a=u(u-1)$ the coprime factors give the two ordinary simple summands directly. Their two $K_0$ classes exhibit why lower monic coefficients require the separate deformation scope, not a highest-weight graded identification.''',['def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight','def-divided-power-klr-idempotents-and-projectives','thm-cyclotomic-klr-algebras-are-finite-dimensional-graded-symmetric',FINITE,'def-cyclotomic-klr-diagram-duality-and-graded-module-duals']))
id='ex-the-categorical-sl-two-commutator-in-a-small-weight';i=next(i for p in pages for i in p['items'] if i['id']==id);done.append(replace(pages,id,i['statement'],r'''At $\lambda=2$ the rank-one nil-Hecke quotient calculation in [[ex-cyclotomic-klr-quotients-in-ranks-zero-one-and-two]] gives precisely the three nonzero blocks at roots $0,\alpha_i,2\alpha_i$, with higher blocks zero. Basic restriction of $F_i k=k[x]/x^2$ is the vector space $k\oplus k\{2\}$; restriction of the vacuum is zero. The actual categorical commutator at target weight $h=2$ has identity shifts $0,2$, so its vacuum evaluation is exactly this direct sum. Projective $E$ on input root $\alpha_i$ carries $q^{-1}$, yielding $q^{-1}(1+q^2)=q^{-1}+q$. The KK module $F$ from the vacuum also carries $q^{-1}$ while KK $E$ is raw, giving the same commutator. The canonical module action instead shifts $E$ and leaves $F$ raw, also giving the same result. All agree with $(q^2-q^{-2})/(q-q^{-1})$. The vanishing is the proved pure rank-one finite-string calculation, not a false propagation inference from a single higher-degree dot polynomial.''',sorted(set(i['deps']+[EX]))))
id='ex-projective-simple-cartan-pairing-in-a-small-cyclotomic-block';done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''For rank one $d_i=1$, $\lambda=2$, let $B_r=R^\Lambda(r\alpha_i)$. The normalized simples are $S_0=k$, $S_1=k$ with $x=0$, and $S_2=C\{-1\}$ of graded dimension $q^{-1}+q$, where $C=k[x_1,x_2]/(x_1+x_2,x_1x_2)$. Their normalized projective covers are $P_0=k$, $P_1=k[x]/x^2$, $P_2=S_2$. Then $T(P_r,S_s)=\delta_{rs}$, with distinct roots paired by zero. Under the integral projective identification $P_0,P_1,P_2$ map to $v,Fv,F^{(2)}v$, respectively, while $[B_2]=(q+q^{-1})[P_2]$ maps to $F^2v$. In $G_0$ the correct regular classes are $[B_1]=(1+q^2)[S_1]$ and $[B_2]=(q+q^{-1})[S_2]$. The canonical restricted-dual map sends $S_r$ to the form-dual of the corresponding divided lower vector; it does not identify $B_1$ with $S_1$.''',r'''The preceding rank-one example supplies $B_1$ and the centered polynomial column for $B_2$. The first regular module has two composition factors $S_1,S_1\{2\}$, giving $1+q^2$. The second block is the endomorphism matrix algebra of the graded column $S_2$, so its regular left module is the column tensored with its graded vector dual: the latter has dimension $q+q^{-1}$, yielding the stated shifted-column decomposition in both groups. The normalized cover-to-head Hom space is $k$ in degree zero and there are no other simple types in a block; quotient-cover evaluation identifies its tensor pairing with this value. The divided-power idempotent identifies $P_2$ with the actual second divided induction, hence $[B_2]=[2]_q[P_2]$ and $F^2=[2]_qF^{(2)}$. This is a cover/simple pairing, not a Cartan matrix claimed to be all ones or a trace that removes a denominator. The integral and dual-module theorems give the stated two identifications.''',[EX,COVER,KZERO,GZERO,'def-the-klr-cartan-pairing','def-divided-power-klr-idempotents-and-projectives']))
id='ex-self-dual-shift-normalization-for-a-cyclotomic-projective';done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''For rank one $d_i=1$ and $\lambda\ge1$, let $B=k[x]/x^\lambda$ and $P=B$. The PROJECTIVE Hom dual satisfies $P^\#\cong P$, so the canonical normalized projective is $P$ with no shift. Its finite vector dual is different:
$$D_\psi P\cong P\{-2(\lambda-1)\}.$$
The unique vector-dual-centered shift of this regular module is $P\{-(\lambda-1)\}$. For $\lambda=1$ both centers agree; for $\lambda=2$, projective Hom-self-duality centers $P$ at $0$ while vector-self-duality centers it at $-1$. If $\lambda=0$ the block is zero and there is no indecomposable projective here. For any normalized cyclotomic projective the general relation is $D_\psi P\cong P^\#\{-2\operatorname{def}_\Lambda(\beta)\}$; it does not redefine the lower-basis projective normalization.''',r'''Evaluation $\operatorname{HOM}_B(B,B)\cong B$ gives the projective dual directly, with the generator-fixing anti-involution producing the same left module. The coefficient-of-$x^{\lambda-1}$ trace has degree $-2(\lambda-1)$ and its perfect anti-diagonal pairing identifies $D_\psi B$ with $B$ in that NEGATIVE shift. Since $D_\psi(P\{s\})=D_\psi P\{-s\}$, vector self-duality solves $-2(\lambda-1)-s=s$, namely $s=-(\lambda-1)$. Projective Hom self-duality instead solves $-s=s$, namely $s=0$. These distinct finite calculations establish uniqueness of each center and their stated examples. The symmetric-algebra theorem gives the general defect shift after the independent projective normalization; no trace halving is used to pick the lower-basis class.''',['def-cyclotomic-klr-diagram-duality-and-graded-module-duals','def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight','thm-cyclotomic-klr-algebras-are-finite-dimensional-graded-symmetric']))
